derivations.claim:
**The actual rank-two matrix-algebra argument.** Let $A\subseteq M_2(\mathbb C)$ be a unital subalgebra acting irreducibly. It contains a nonscalar element, since scalar matrices preserve every line. If some $T\in A$ has distinct eigenvalues, conjugate to an eigenbasis. Its two rank-one spectral projections belong to $A$, being degree-one polynomials in $T$, so $E_{11},E_{22}\in A$. Irreducibility forces an element $B\in A$ with $b_{12}
e0$, since otherwise every element preserves the second coordinate line; it also forces $C\in A$ with $c_{21}
e0$, since otherwise every element preserves the first coordinate line. Thus $E_{11}BE_{22}=b_{12}E_{12}$ and $E_{22}CE_{11}=c_{21}E_{21}$ put all four matrix units in $A$. If no element initially has distinct eigenvalues, take a nonscalar $T$ and subtract its repeated eigenvalue. The resulting nonzero nilpotent is conjugate, after scaling one basis vector, to $N=E_{12}$. Irreducibility again supplies $B$ with $b_{21}
e0$. Then $NB=\left( egin{smallmatrix}b_{21}&b_{22}\0&0\end{smallmatrix}ight)$ has distinct eigenvalues $b_{21},0$ and reduces to the previous case. Hence every such algebra is $M_2(\mathbb C)$. In particular an irreducible group image spans the full matrix algebra by [F1].
